REGIONAL REGULATION OF BLITAR REGENCY

NUMBER 11 OF 2013

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF BLITAR,

Considering:

a. that women and children are creatures created by Almighty God who have noble rights and dignity in accordance with their nature as human beings;
b. that there are still many women and children who do not fully receive their basic rights, especially women and children who are victims of violence;
c. that all forms of acts of violence against women and children constitute violations of Human Rights, so that women and children who are victims of violence need to receive protection;
d. that the protection of women and children, especially victims of violence, is one of the obligations that must be carried out by the Regional Government as one of the efforts to realize community welfare;
e. that to realize protection for women and children who are victims of violence in an integrated and sustainable manner, institutional support and legislation are needed that can guarantee its implementation in a coordinated and directed manner;
f. that based on considerations a, b, c, d, and e, it is necessary to immediately establish a Regional Regulation of Blitar Regency concerning the Protection of Women and Children.

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution;
2. Law Number 12 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within the Province of East Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1950 Number 41), as amended by Law Number 2 of 1965 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1965 Number 19, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3851);
3. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019);
4. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
5. Law Number 3 of 1997 concerning Juvenile Court (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1997 Number 3, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3668); there is already a newer one Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Justice System
6. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention No. 138 Concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment (Convention concerning Minimum Age for Children Allowed to Work) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
7. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
8. Law Number 1 of 2000 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention No. 182 Concerning The Prohibition and Immediate Action for The Elimination of The Worst Forms of Child Labour (ILO Convention Number 182 concerning the Prohibition and Immediate Action for the Elimination of the Worst Forms of Child Labor) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2000 Number 30, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3941));
9. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
10. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);
11. Law Number 20 of 2003 concerning the National Education System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2003 Number 78, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4301);
12. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2011 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 4419);
13. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as has been amended several times, most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
14. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims) State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
15. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
16. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare;
17. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2011 Number 82);
18. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System;
19. Government Regulation Number 79 of 2005 concerning Guidelines for Guidance and Supervision of Regional Government Administration;
20. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning the Division of Government Affairs between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
21. Government Regulation Number 39 of 2012 concerning the Implementation of Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2012 Number 68, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5294);
22. Presidential Decree Number 36 of 1990 concerning Ratification of the Convention On the Rights of the Child (Convention on the Rights of the Child) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1990 Number 57);
23. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 6 of 2009 concerning Gender Disaggregated Data and Mainstreaming of Children's Rights;
24. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Fulfillment Number 11 of 2011 concerning Policy for the Development of Child-Friendly Regencies/Cities;
25. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
26. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 15 of 2008 concerning General Guidelines for the Preparation of Gender Mainstreaming (PUG) in Development as amended by Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 67 of 2011 concerning Amendment to Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 15 of 2008 concerning General Guidelines for the Preparation of Gender Mainstreaming (PUG) in Development;
27. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 1 of 2014 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products;
28. Regional Regulation of Blitar Regency Number 5 of 2011 concerning Amendment to Regional Regulation Number 19 of 2008 concerning Organization and Work Procedures of Regional Agencies.

With Joint Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF BLITAR REGENCY

and

REGENT OF BLITAR

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

CHAPTER I

GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1

In this Regional Regulation, the following terms shall mean:

1. Region is Blitar Regency;
2. Regional House of Representatives of Blitar Regency hereinafter abbreviated as DPRD is the DPRD of Blitar Regency;
3. Regent is the Regent of Blitar;
4. Regional Government is the Government of Blitar Regency; 5. Community is individuals, families, groups and social organizations and/or community organizations;
6. Woman is a creature created by God with female sex, or with reproductive organs in the form of ovaries or ovum, uterus, and hymen;
7. Child is a person who has not reached the age of 18 (eighteen) years, including a child still in the womb;
8. Protection of women and children is all efforts to protect women and children, starting from prevention, safeguarding and handling, aimed at providing a sense of security and comfort to women and children and their families, especially those who are victims of violence;
9. Victim of violence is a woman and child who experiences violence and/or threat of violence whether physical, psychological, sexual or economic both in the domestic and public sphere;
10. Violence is any act or omission of obligation that results in misery or suffering whether physical, mental, sexual and economic including threats to commit acts, coercion or deprivation of freedom unlawfully;
11. Neglect is an act that results in economic dependence of a person on another person in one household;
12. Prevention is all efforts aimed at preventing the occurrence of violence and threats of violence;
13. Safeguarding is all efforts so that women and children who are vulnerable to violence do not become victims of violence;
14. Handling is all efforts aimed at handling cases of violence against women and children and their families including litigation and non-litigation services;
15. Trafficking in persons is the act of recruitment, transportation, harboring, sending, transfer, or receipt of a person by threat of violence, use of violence, kidnapping, confinement, forgery, fraud, abuse of power or vulnerable position, debt bondage or giving payment or benefits, so as to obtain consent from the person holding control over that other person, whether conducted within the country or between countries, for the purpose of exploitation or resulting in the person being exploited.
16. Sexual exploitation is any form of utilization of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain profit, but not limited to all activities of prostitution and indecency.
17. Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A) is one form of service facility for women and children consisting of government and non-government elements in an effort to fulfill information and needs in the fields of education, health, economy, politics, law, fulfillment and prevention of acts of violence and trafficking against women and children.
18. Safe house is a temporary residence used to provide fulfillment to victims in accordance with the determined standard operating procedures.
19. Child in normal or regular situation is a child who gets the opportunity to grow and develop optimally.
20. Child as victim of violence is a child who becomes a victim of one or all types of violence, whether physical, psychological, sexual, or neglected by parents and family including victims of human trafficking;
21. Child with disability is a child with a disability;
22. Parent is biological father and/or mother, or step father and/or mother, adoptive father and/or mother;
23. Family is the smallest unit in society consisting of husband and wife, or husband and wife and their children, or father and children, or mother and children, or blood relatives in direct line upward or downward up to the third degree;
24. Guardian is a person or body who in fact exercises parental authority as a parent over a child;
25. Minimum Service Standard (SPM) is a performance benchmark for integrated service units in providing complaint handling services, health services, social rehabilitation, law enforcement and legal assistance as well as repatriation-social reintegration especially for women and children victims of violence;
26. Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) is a technical guideline for the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence prepared based on Minimum Service Standards according to regional financial capacity.
27. Neglected child is a child whose needs are not properly fulfilled by those obligated according to their ability whether physical, mental and spiritual.
28. Related SKPD is an SKPD whose main tasks and functions handle the protection of women and children.
29. SKPD in charge of regional development planning is the Regional Development Planning Agency of Blitar Regency, hereinafter abbreviated as BAPEDA of Blitar Regency.
30. SKPD in charge of women empowerment and child protection is the Agency for Women Empowerment and Family Planning of Blitar Regency.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES, FOUNDATION AND PRINCIPLES

Article 2

The implementation of Protection of Women and Children is based on Pancasila and founded on the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, and is based on the following basic principles:
a. respect for human rights, women's human rights and children's rights;
b. gender justice and equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. protection for confidentiality, security and comfort of victims;
e. the best interests of the child;
f. the right to life, survival, growth and development, and respect for the child's opinion.

Article 9

1. Forms of handling services for victims of violence organized by P2TP2A include:
a. medical services in the form of treatment and recovery of physical condition carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medicolegal services which are a form of medical services, especially Visum et Repertum and Visum et Repertum Psichiatricum, for evidentiary purposes in the judicial process, starting from the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court levels;
c. legal aid services to assist victims of violence in undergoing the judicial process;
d. spiritual services for moral strengthening according to the religion and beliefs adhered to;
e. first psychosocial assistance to victims carried out to identify the occurrence of violence and trauma experienced by victims;
f. accompaniment of victims of violence carried out by competent persons or institutions who have the expertise to conduct counseling, therapy and advocacy for the strengthening and recovery of victims of violence and have cooperated with P2TP2T or other names;
g. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and provision of economic access so that victims can be economically independent; and
h. services to return and or remain in educational institutions for child victims of violence.
2. The implementation of handling services for women and children victims of violence shall be carried out: quickly, safely and comfortably, with empathy, non-discrimination, easily accessible, free of charge and guaranteed confidentiality.
3. The mechanisms and technical procedures for implementing handling services for women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be organized according to Standard Operating Procedures (SOP) and further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 11

Regional Government is obliged and responsible to:
a. build a system for implementing protection of women and children, especially victims of violence, and their families;
b. provide infrastructure including safe houses/shelters, Human Resources comprising lawyers, doctors and psychologists-psychiatrists, volunteer companions, spiritual figures, economists, and social workers;
c. increase the institutional capacity of protection organizers, victims of violence especially for women, children and their families;
d. build a data and information center on protection, victims of violence especially for women, children and their families;
e. conduct monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection, victims of violence especially for women, children and their families.

Article 12

In carrying out its obligations and responsibilities, the Regional Government has the authority to:
a. make policies and programs;
b. formulate implementation guidelines;
c. organize services;
d. coordinate policies, programs, budgets and services;
e. conduct monitoring, supervision and evaluation of the implementation of protection and prevention of victims of violence especially for women, children and their families.

CHAPTER IX

INVESTIGATION PROVISIONS

FOR CIVIL SERVANTS

Article 18

1. Certain civil servant officials within the Regency Government are given authority to carry out investigations into violations of the provisions in this Regional Regulation.
2. The authority of investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) is:
a. receiving reports or complaints from a person or family and other persons, regarding the existence of actions or violations of Regional Regulations;
b. conducting first actions and examinations at the scene;
c. summoning experts required in connection with case examination;
d. summoning persons to be heard for their statements and examined as witnesses or suspects;
e. conducting actions according to law that can be accounted for.
